md`# brain-logs

A Cloud Brain recipe: the logs of this Brain's Workers, read from Cloudflare Workers Logs. It keeps nothing and has no query language of its own. A body is passed to Cloudflare's telemetry API and the answer is returned as Cloudflare gave it, its errors and limits included.

| Method | Cloudflare endpoint |
|---|---|
| \`logs.query\` | \`POST /accounts/ACCOUNT/workers/observability/telemetry/query\`: events, invocations, traces or calculations |
| \`logs.keys\` | \`…/telemetry/keys\`: the field names seen in a time range |
| \`logs.values\` | \`…/telemetry/values\`: the values of one field |
| \`logs.deploys?from=&to=\` | \`…/telemetry/query\`, with a body of the service's own: what the deployer wrote about deploys, and the test runs counted. See "The deploys of a span" |

The body is Cloudflare's: [the query API](https://developers.cloudflare.com/api/resources/workers/subresources/observability/subresources/telemetry/methods/query/). The service changes two things in it (\`logsScope\`). \`datasets\` is \`["cloudflare-workers"]\`. The filters are put inside one filter on the script name, joined with AND, so a query reads only the Workers of this Brain: the base name, and every name that starts with the base name and a hyphen. A base name therefore has no hyphen: the Brain \`cb4\` would read the scripts of a Brain \`cb4-test\` on the same account. The installer refuses such a name (\`installBrain\`, \`brain.ts\`); nothing checks a Brain installed before 2026-10-08. With \`?worker=brain-x-NAME\` in the address the filter is that one Worker.

\`\`\`js
// the core's line of one call. The answer had cf-ray: a47789bfbccce517-FRA; the value is the part before the hyphen.
POST /xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.logs.query
{ "queryId": "ray", "timeframe": { "from": 1791480000000, "to": 1791483600000 }, "view": "events", "limit": 100,
  "parameters": { "filters": [{ "key": "ray", "operation": "eq", "type": "string", "value": "a47789bfbccce517" }] } }
// each call of one method. method is the name after com.lopecode.brain., not the whole NSID.
  "parameters": { "filters": [{ "key": "method", "operation": "eq", "type": "string", "value": "bluesky.poll" }] } }
\`\`\`

\`queryId\` is required by Cloudflare and is any text. The id of a query saved on the account does not replace the body: with the id of a saved query that matches nothing, the answer was the lines the body asked for, inside this Brain's filter (cb4, 2026-10-08).

## What is in the logs

Cloudflare keeps what a Worker writes with \`console.log\`, 7 days on Workers Paid and 3 on Free, and gives every line \`$metadata.traceId\`, which is the same in each Worker one call passed through. The deployer turns Workers Logs on for every Worker and Cloudflare's own line per invocation off, because that line stores request headers and the whole URL. With it off, Cloudflare still stores, beside each line a Worker writes, the method and URL of that call with its query string (\`$workers.event.request\`: \`method\`, \`url\`, \`path\`, \`search\`), not its headers or body. So a parameter of a method is in the logs, and a long-lived secret must not be put in a path or a query string. A short-lived single-use code may be. One exception is known: Meta sends the long-lived \`hub.verify_token\` in the query string of \`/hooks/whatsapp\`, and the core writes a line for that call. Cloudflare wrote \`REDACTED\` for that name on a scratch Worker (2026-10-08); that was not seen on a Brain.

| Line | Written by | Fields |
|---|---|---|
| \`at: "call"\` | the core, one a call | \`ray caller via origin delegation holder method worker status error ms price rule by\` (see \`coreApp\` in brain-core) |
| \`at: "throw"\` | the wrapper, when a service throws | \`worker ray error stack\`: the error's name and the frames of its stack, not its message, which can quote what a caller sent |
| \`at: "deploy.…"\`, \`"token.…"\` | the deployer | \`target hash version previous first last reason token groups\`. \`reason\` and \`last\` can hold Cloudflare's text or a health check's answer about the code deployed, not what a caller of a method sent |
| \`at: "browser.…"\` | brain-browser | \`session reason\` |
| any | a service, with the \`log\` cell | what it gives, with \`worker\` and \`ray\` |

\`ray\` is the \`cf-ray\` header of the answer a caller received, without the hyphen and the letters after it (\`a47789bfbccce517-FRA\` is logged as \`a47789bfbccce517\`): a Worker sees the id with no data-centre code. \`method\` is the name after \`com.lopecode.brain.\`, the path pattern a service declared, or \`(unknown)\` for a name nobody declared. A line of the kernel is found by \`$metadata.rayId\`, and every line of the call by the \`traceId\` of that line.

## The deploys of a span

\`GET logs.deploys?from=MS&to=MS\` answers the lines the deployer wrote with \`at: "deploy.…"\` between two times in milliseconds: with neither, the 24 hours before now; 7 days at most, which is what Cloudflare keeps. A caller gives the span and nothing else. The filter is fixed (the deployer's script, \`at\` starting \`deploy.\`) and is checked again on each line of the answer.

\`\`\`js
{ from, to, queries,                       // queries: how many Cloudflare was asked
  lines: [{ at, target, hash, …, t }],     // each line as the deployer wrote it, oldest first; t is Cloudflare's time, ms
  workers: [{ worker, runs, verified, failed, none, stale, putBack, hashes: [{ hash, verified, failed }] }],
  tests: [{ worker, test, fails, runs, passedOnSameHash, passedLater, hashes, last }] }
\`\`\`

A line is the deployer's own object and its time. What Cloudflare stores beside it (the URL and path of the call, the ids) is left out. \`reason\` of \`deploy.reason\` is what whoever deployed gave as the reason; \`run\` of \`deploy.verify\` is the address the tests ran at.

**Whole, not sampled.** Cloudflare samples a wide \`events\` query: 5 of the \`deploy.verified\` lines of 2 days came back with \`statistics.abr_level: 10\`, where one-hour windows gave 35 for 3.5 of those hours with \`abr_level: 1\` (cb4, 2026-10-11). So \`deployLines\` asks for the span, and asks again for the two halves of any window whose answer has an \`abr_level\` other than 1 or as many lines as the limit of 2000. Halves share their middle and a line is kept once, by Cloudflare's id. A minute that is still not whole, or a span that needs more than 200 queries, is answered 502 and no lines.

**What is counted** (\`deployRuns\`). A run is a \`deploy.verified\` or a \`deploy.failed\` line: the tests of one Worker at one hash, run by the deployer after a deploy. A put-back whose reason starts \`its tests failed:\` with no \`deploy.failed\` line of that Worker and hash before it is a failed run as well, with the first test its reason names and no other: the put-back of brain-x-issues at 05:50:48 UTC on 2026-10-11 has no such line, its verdict came from a page. \`none\` and \`stale\` are runs that gave no verdict for the hash deployed, \`putBack\` every put-back, for a test or not. A line of \`tests\` is one test that failed in the span:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| \`fails\` | the \`deploy.failed\` lines that name it |
| \`runs\` | the runs of its Worker in the span. The lines do not name the tests that passed, so this is every run of the Worker, also those from before the test was written |
| \`passedOnSameHash\` | fails at a hash that also has a \`verified\` run in the span: the same code passed and failed, which is what a flaky test is (Parry et al., *A Survey of Flaky Tests*, TOSEM 2021). A failed version is put back at once, so a hash is seldom run twice |
| \`passedLater\` | the other fails that a \`verified\` run of the Worker at another hash followed: fixed, or flaky; the lines cannot say which |

So \`fails / runs\` is a fail rate, not a flake rate: a fail at a new hash may be the defect the run is there to catch. Not in the lines: the names of the tests that passed, the browser, and a failure's message past the 200 characters of the put-back's \`reason\`.

## Who can call

\`logs.query\`, \`logs.keys\` and \`logs.values\`: the owner in their own session. The owner can set another rule (\`rule.put\`). To let a Worker read its own lines and no other's: \`caller.kind == "worker" && request.params.worker == caller.worker\` for \`com.lopecode.brain.logs.query\`.

\`logs.deploys\`: the owner, or a token of the owner that names it (\`caller.id == "owner"\`; a token's call is the owner's with no tab). The owner, 2026-10-11: "We should allow deployment log access for things that deploy and fix things because it is vital information". The deploy lines hold what the deployer wrote about a Worker and nothing a caller of a method sent; the core's line of each call and a service's own lines name callers, and stay with the three methods above. A token for it is made once with the owner's session: \`brain.ts token loop-deploys logs.deploys\`, then \`brain.ts curl "/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.logs.deploys?from=MS&to=MS" --as loop-deploys\`.

## The Cloudflare token

The service declares \`cloudflare: ["Workers Observability Read"]\`. The deployer holds such a deploy for the owner, mints a Cloudflare API token with that one permission and binds it to this Worker alone. The \`cloudflareApi\` cell sends it. The token reads the logs of every Worker on the Cloudflare account, which is why \`logsScope\` is not optional and no parameter can remove it.

Calls have no price: Cloudflare states none for a query. Its limit is the account's API limit, 1200 requests in 5 minutes, and an answer over it is passed on.`
// %%
// The script on Cloudflare of a Worker of this Brain: brain-x-echo on the Brain cb4 is cb4-x-echo.
logsScript = (base, worker) => (/^brain(-[a-z0-9-]{1,60})?$/.test(worker) ? base + worker.slice("brain".length) : null)
// %%
// A body with this Brain's filter around the caller's. `what` is query, keys or values.
logsScope = (body, what, base, worker = null) => {
  const name = (operation, value) => ({ key: "$workers.scriptName", operation, type: "string", value });
  const mine = worker ? name("eq", logsScript(base, worker)) : { kind: "group", filterCombination: "or", filters: [name("eq", base), name("starts_with", base + "-")] };
  const given = (what === "query" ? body.parameters : body) || {};
  const theirs = Array.isArray(given.filters) && given.filters.length ? [{ kind: "group", filterCombination: given.filterCombination || "and", filters: given.filters }] : [];
  const scoped = { ...given, datasets: ["cloudflare-workers"], filterCombination: "and", filters: [mine, ...theirs] };
  return what === "query" ? { ...body, parameters: scoped } : scoped;
}
// %%
// The lines the deployer wrote about deploys between two times (ms), whole and oldest first. `ask` sends one
// telemetry query and gives Cloudflare's `result`. A window that was sampled (abr_level not 1) or that answered as
// many lines as it may is asked again as two halves, which share their middle: a line is kept once, by Cloudflare's id.
deployLines = async (ask, base, from, to, { limit = 2000, queries = 200 } = {}) => {
  const script = logsScript(base, "brain-deployer"), kept = new Map();
  let asked = 0;
  const read = async (a, b) => {
    if (asked >= queries) throw new Error(`more than ${queries} queries`);
    asked++;
    const result = await ask(logsScope({ queryId: "deploys", timeframe: { from: a, to: b }, view: "events", limit, parameters: { filters: [{ key: "at", operation: "starts_with", type: "string", value: "deploy." }] } }, "query", base, "brain-deployer"));
    const events = (result && result.events && result.events.events) || [];
    const level = result && result.run && result.run.statistics ? result.run.statistics.abr_level : undefined;
    if (level !== 1 || events.length >= limit) {
      if (b - a <= 60000) throw new Error(`the minute from ${a} is not whole: ${events.length} lines, abr_level ${level}`);
      const mid = a + Math.floor((b - a) / 2);
      return void (await Promise.all([read(a, mid), read(mid, b)]));
    }
    for (const e of events) {
      // The filter again, on the answer: only the deployer's own deploy lines leave, without what Cloudflare keeps beside them.
      const s = e && e.source;
      if (!s || typeof s !== "object" || typeof s.at !== "string" || !s.at.startsWith("deploy.") || !e.$workers || e.$workers.scriptName !== script) continue;
      const id = String((e.$metadata && e.$metadata.id) || JSON.stringify([e.timestamp, s]));
      kept.set(id, { id, line: { ...s, t: e.timestamp } });
    }
  };
  await read(from, to);
  return [...kept.values()].sort((x, y) => x.line.t - y.line.t || (x.id < y.id ? -1 : 1)).map((k) => k.line);
}
// %%
// The test runs in the deployer's lines, counted by Worker and hash, and each test that failed. See "What is counted".
deployRuns = (lines) => {
  const kinds = { "deploy.verified": "verified", "deploy.failed": "failed", "deploy.verify.none": "none", "deploy.verify.stale": "stale", "deploy.put-back": "putBack" };
  const workers = new Map(), fails = [];
  for (const l of [...lines].sort((a, b) => a.t - b.t)) {
    const kind = l && typeof l.target === "string" ? kinds[l.at] : undefined;
    if (!kind) continue;
    if (!workers.has(l.target)) workers.set(l.target, { worker: l.target, runs: 0, verified: 0, failed: 0, none: 0, stale: 0, putBack: 0, hashes: [] });
    const w = workers.get(l.target);
    w[kind]++;
    // A put-back on a test follows its deploy.failed line. One with none before it is a failed run too: the verdict
    // came from a page and not from the deployer's own run, and the reason names the first test that failed.
    const told = kind === "putBack" && /^its tests failed: /.test(String(l.reason));
    if (kind !== "verified" && kind !== "failed" && !told) continue;
    let h = w.hashes.find((x) => x.hash === l.hash);
    if (!h) w.hashes.push((h = { hash: l.hash, verified: 0, failed: 0, passed: [], unanswered: 0 }));
    if (told && h.unanswered > 0) { h.unanswered--; continue; }
    w.runs++;
    if (kind === "verified") { h.verified++; h.passed.push(l.t); continue; }
    h.failed++;
    if (told) w.failed++;
    else h.unanswered++;
    const named = told ? (/^its tests failed: ([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*): /.exec(l.reason) || []).slice(1) : Array.isArray(l.failed) ? l.failed : [];
    for (const test of named) fails.push({ worker: l.target, test: String(test), hash: l.hash, t: l.t });
  }
  const tests = new Map();
  for (const f of fails) {
    const w = workers.get(f.worker), key = JSON.stringify([f.worker, f.test]);
    if (!tests.has(key)) tests.set(key, { worker: f.worker, test: f.test, fails: 0, runs: w.runs, passedOnSameHash: 0, passedLater: 0, hashes: [], last: f.t });
    const t = tests.get(key);
    t.fails++;
    t.last = f.t;
    if (!t.hashes.includes(f.hash)) t.hashes.push(f.hash);
    if (w.hashes.find((h) => h.hash === f.hash).verified) t.passedOnSameHash++;
    else if (w.hashes.some((h) => h.passed.some((at) => at > f.t))) t.passedLater++;
  }
  const by = (a, b) => (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0);
  return {
    workers: [...workers.values()].sort((a, b) => by(a.worker, b.worker)).map((w) => ({ ...w, hashes: w.hashes.map(({ hash, verified, failed }) => ({ hash, verified, failed })) })),
    tests: [...tests.values()].sort((a, b) => b.fails - a.fails || by(a.worker, b.worker) || by(a.test, b.test))
  };
}
// %%
logs_service = cloudflare.Service(
  "logs",
  async (request) => {
    const json = (status, error, message) => new Response(JSON.stringify({ error, message }), { status, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } });
    const url = new URL(request.url), what = url.pathname.split(".").pop(), worker = url.searchParams.get("worker");
    if (what === "deploys") {
      const given = (name, or) => (url.searchParams.has(name) ? (/^\d{1,15}$/.test(url.searchParams.get(name)) ? Number(url.searchParams.get(name)) : NaN) : or);
      const to = given("to", Date.now()), from = given("from", to - 86400000);
      if (!(from < to && to - from <= 7 * 86400000)) return json(400, "InvalidRequest", "from and to are times in milliseconds, from before to, 7 days apart at most");
      let queries = 0;
      const ask = async (query) => {
        queries++;
        const r = await cloudflareApi.fetch("/workers/observability/telemetry/query", { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(query) });
        const data = await r.json().catch(() => null);
        if (!r.ok || !data || !data.result) throw new Error(`Cloudflare answered ${r.status} ${JSON.stringify((data && data.errors) || null).slice(0, 300)}`);
        return data.result;
      };
      try {
        const lines = await deployLines(ask, config.base, from, to);
        return new Response(JSON.stringify({ from, to, queries, lines, ...deployRuns(lines) }), { headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } });
      } catch (e) {
        return json(502, "UpstreamFailure", String((e && e.message) || e));
      }
    }
    if (!["query", "keys", "values"].includes(what)) return json(404, "MethodNotImplemented", "logs.query, logs.keys, logs.values and logs.deploys");
    const body = await request.json().catch(() => null);
    if (!body || typeof body !== "object" || Array.isArray(body)) return json(400, "InvalidRequest", "the body is the body of Cloudflare's telemetry " + what);
    if (worker !== null && !logsScript(config.base, worker)) return json(400, "InvalidRequest", "worker is the name of a Worker of this Brain: brain, brain-core, brain-x-NAME");
    const r = await cloudflareApi.fetch("/workers/observability/telemetry/" + what, { method: "POST", body: JSON.stringify(logsScope(body, what, config.base, worker)) });
    return new Response(r.body, { status: r.status, headers: { "content-type": r.headers.get("content-type") || "application/json" } });
  },
  {
    cloudflare: ["Workers Observability Read"],
    methods: {
      // The owner, or a token of the owner that names it: the deployer's lines about deploys hold nothing a caller sent.
      "com.lopecode.brain.logs.deploys": { type: "query", allow: 'caller.id == "owner"' },
      "com.lopecode.brain.logs.query": { type: "procedure", allow: "caller.session" },
      "com.lopecode.brain.logs.keys": { type: "procedure", allow: "caller.session" },
      "com.lopecode.brain.logs.values": { type: "procedure", allow: "caller.session" }
    }
  }
)
// %%
logs_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", logs_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced logs_service";
}
// %%
md`## Tests`
// %%
logsRig = async () => {
  const seen = [];
  const sim = await simulate(logs_service, {
    secrets: { CF_TOKEN: "minted-1", BRAIN_KEY: "k" },
    config: { base: "cb4", account: "acct" },
    fetch: async (request) => {
      seen.push({ url: request.url, authorization: request.headers.get("authorization"), body: await request.json() });
      return seen.length === 3
        ? new Response('{"success":false,"errors":[{"code":10000,"message":"rate limited"}]}', { status: 429, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } })
        : new Response('{"success":true,"result":{"events":{"count":1}}}', { headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } });
    }
  });
  const call = async (name, body) => {
    const r = await sim.fetch("https://logs.internal/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.logs." + name, { method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "x-brain-caller": "owner" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) });
    return { status: r.status, data: await r.json() };
  };
  return { sim, seen, call };
}
// %%
test_a_query_is_passed_on_inside_this_brains_filter = {
  const rig = await logsRig();
  const scope = { kind: "group", filterCombination: "or", filters: [{ key: "$workers.scriptName", operation: "eq", type: "string", value: "cb4" }, { key: "$workers.scriptName", operation: "starts_with", type: "string", value: "cb4-" }] };
  const theirs = [{ key: "status", operation: "gte", type: "number", value: 400 }, { key: "$workers.scriptName", operation: "eq", type: "string", value: "another-brain" }];
  // The caller asks for another dataset and joins with OR. Their filters stay together, inside this Brain's.
  const out = await rig.call("query", { queryId: "q", timeframe: { from: 1, to: 2 }, view: "events", limit: 5, parameters: { datasets: ["another"], filterCombination: "or", filters: theirs, needle: { value: "x" } } });
  expect(out).toEqual({ status: 200, data: { success: true, result: { events: { count: 1 } } } });
  expect(rig.seen[0]).toEqual({
    url: "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/accounts/acct/workers/observability/telemetry/query",
    authorization: "Bearer minted-1",
    body: { queryId: "q", timeframe: { from: 1, to: 2 }, view: "events", limit: 5, parameters: { datasets: ["cloudflare-workers"], filterCombination: "and", filters: [scope, { kind: "group", filterCombination: "or", filters: theirs }], needle: { value: "x" } } }
  });
  // keys and values have their filters at the top.
  await rig.call("keys", { timeframe: { from: 1, to: 2 }, limit: 9 });
  expect(rig.seen[1].url).toMatch(/telemetry\/keys$/);
  expect(rig.seen[1].body).toEqual({ timeframe: { from: 1, to: 2 }, limit: 9, datasets: ["cloudflare-workers"], filterCombination: "and", filters: [scope] });
  // Cloudflare's refusal is the answer.
  expect(await rig.call("values", { key: "at", type: "string" })).toEqual({ status: 429, data: { success: false, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "rate limited" }] } });
  return "3 calls";
}
// %%
test_worker_narrows_a_query_to_one_worker_of_this_brain = {
  const rig = await logsRig();
  const one = async (worker, body = { parameters: {} }) => {
    const r = await rig.sim.fetch("https://logs.internal/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.logs.query?worker=" + encodeURIComponent(worker), { method: "POST", headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "x-brain-caller": "worker:brain-x-echo" }, body: JSON.stringify(body) });
    return r.status;
  };
  expect(await one("brain-x-echo")).toBe(200);
  expect(rig.seen[0].body.parameters.filters).toEqual([{ key: "$workers.scriptName", operation: "eq", type: "string", value: "cb4-x-echo" }]);
  expect(await one("brain")).toBe(200);
  expect(rig.seen[1].body.parameters.filters[0].value).toBe("cb4");
  // A name that is not a Worker of a Brain is refused before Cloudflare is asked.
  for (const bad of ["cb9-core", "", "brain-x-a b", "brainiac", "other"]) expect(await one(bad)).toBe(400);
  expect(rig.seen.length).toBe(2);
  // Only query, keys and values go to Cloudflare.
  for (const other of ["run", "destinations", "query/../../scripts"]) expect((await rig.call(other, { parameters: {} })).status).toBe(404);
  expect(rig.seen.length).toBe(2);
  expect(logsScript("cb4", "brain-m-0123456789-todo")).toBe("cb4-m-0123456789-todo");
  return "2 passed on, 5 refused";
}
// %%
test_logs_declares_one_cloudflare_permission_and_the_owners_session = {
  const { meta, parts } = await logs_service.emit();
  expect(meta.worker).toBe("brain-x-logs");
  expect(meta.cloudflare).toEqual(["Workers Observability Read"]);
  expect([meta.resources, meta.secrets, meta.calls]).toEqual([[], [], []]);
  expect(Object.entries(meta.access).map(([nsid, a]) => [nsid.split(".").pop(), a.type, a.allow])).toEqual([["deploys", "query", 'caller.id == "owner"'], ["keys", "procedure", "caller.session"], ["query", "procedure", "caller.session"], ["values", "procedure", "caller.session"]]);
  // The token is read from the binding the deployer made, and from nowhere a caller can set.
  expect(parts[0].text).toContain("__rt.platform.secrets.CF_TOKEN");
  // The owner's rule for a Worker reading its own lines.
  const rule = { allow: 'caller.kind == "worker" && request.params.worker == caller.worker' };
  const ask = (caller, worker) => decide(rule, { caller: callerOf({ caller }), origin: callerOf({ caller }), request: { params: worker === null ? {} : { worker } } }).allow;
  expect([ask("worker:brain-x-echo", "brain-x-echo"), ask("worker:brain-x-echo", "brain-core"), ask("worker:brain-x-echo", null), ask("anonymous", "")]).toEqual([true, false, false, false]);
  // A token of the owner is the owner with no tab: it reads the deploy lines and no others. A member, a Worker and a stranger read neither.
  const may = (allow, who) => decide({ allow }, { caller: callerOf(who), origin: callerOf(who), request: { params: {} } }).allow;
  const callers = [{ caller: "owner", via: "session", tab: true }, { caller: "owner", via: "delegation:b6559fa96c36", holder: "secret:loop-deploys", tab: false }, { caller: "did:plc:member" }, { caller: "worker:brain-x-echo" }, { caller: "anonymous" }];
  expect(callers.map((who) => may(meta.access["com.lopecode.brain.logs.deploys"].allow, who))).toEqual([true, true, false, false, false]);
  expect(callers.map((who) => may(meta.access["com.lopecode.brain.logs.query"].allow, who))).toEqual([true, false, false, false, false]);
  return meta.cloudflare[0];
}
// %%
// Cloudflare's telemetry query as far as logs.deploys reads it: the events of a fixture inside the timeframe, newest
// first, cut at the limit, and sampled (abr_level 10) when the timeframe is longer than `whole` ms.
deploysRig = async (events, { whole = 6 * 3600000, status = 200 } = {}) => {
  const seen = [];
  const answer = (body) => {
    seen.push(body);
    const { from, to } = body.timeframe;
    const inside = events.filter((e) => e.timestamp >= from && e.timestamp <= to).sort((a, b) => b.timestamp - a.timestamp).slice(0, body.limit);
    return { run: { statistics: { abr_level: to - from > whole ? 10 : 1 } }, events: { events: inside, count: inside.length } };
  };
  const sim = await simulate(logs_service, {
    secrets: { CF_TOKEN: "minted-1", BRAIN_KEY: "k" },
    config: { base: "cb4", account: "acct" },
    fetch: async (request) => new Response(JSON.stringify(status === 200 ? { success: true, result: answer(await request.json()) } : { success: false, errors: [{ code: 10000, message: "rate limited" }] }), { status, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" } })
  });
  const get = async (query) => {
    const r = await sim.fetch("https://logs.internal/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.logs.deploys" + query, { headers: { "x-brain-caller": "owner", "x-brain-via": "delegation:b6559fa96c36", "x-brain-holder": "secret:loop-deploys", "x-brain-tab": "0" } });
    return { status: r.status, data: await r.json() };
  };
  return { seen, answer, get };
}
// %%
// A line as Cloudflare answers it. The shape is the answer of cb4 on 2026-10-11 (deploy.failed of brain-x-snapshot).
deployEvent = (timestamp, source, scriptName = "cb4-deployer") => ({
  source: { ...source, deployer: "f9007d2d", worker: "brain-deployer", ray: "a48bd6e14a6acc97" },
  dataset: "cloudflare-workers",
  timestamp,
  $workers: { truncated: false, scriptName, eventType: "fetch", event: { request: { method: "POST", url: "https://cb4-deployer.endpointservices.workers.dev/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.infra.apply?secret=1", path: "/xrpc/com.lopecode.brain.infra.apply" } } },
  $metadata: { id: "id-" + timestamp + "-" + source.at + "-" + source.target, requestId: "c01b", traceId: "4ac2" }
})
// %%
test_deploys_answers_the_deployers_lines_of_a_span_whole = {
  const H = 3600000, from = 1791676800000, to = from + 24 * H;
  const events = [
    deployEvent(from + 1 * H, { at: "deploy.uploaded", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h1", version: "v1" }),
    deployEvent(from + 1 * H + 5, { at: "deploy.verify", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h1", run: "https://cb4.example/#verify=brain-x-echo" }),
    // On the middle of the span, which both halves are asked for.
    deployEvent(from + 12 * H, { at: "deploy.failed", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h1", failed: ["test_a"], otherTestsRan: 132, ms: 8105 }),
    deployEvent(from + 12 * H + 1, { at: "deploy.put-back", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h1", previous: "h0", reason: "its tests failed: test_a: no" }),
    deployEvent(from + 23 * H, { at: "deploy.verified", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h2", tests: 3, otherTestsRan: 132, ms: 900 }),
    // What the fixed filter keeps out, should Cloudflare answer it anyway: another Worker's line, and a line of the deployer about a token.
    deployEvent(from + 2 * H, { at: "deploy.verified", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h9", tests: 1 }, "cb4-x-echo"),
    deployEvent(from + 3 * H, { at: "token.minted", target: "brain-x-logs", token: "t" }),
    { timestamp: from + 4 * H, $workers: { scriptName: "cb4-deployer" }, $metadata: { id: "text" }, source: "deploy.verified as text" }
  ];
  const rig = await deploysRig(events);
  // The caller's filters are not read: the body sent is the service's own.
  const out = await rig.get(`?from=${from}&to=${to}&worker=brain-core&filters=x`);
  expect(out.status).toBe(200);
  expect(rig.seen[0]).toEqual({
    queryId: "deploys",
    timeframe: { from, to },
    view: "events",
    limit: 2000,
    parameters: { datasets: ["cloudflare-workers"], filterCombination: "and", filters: [{ key: "$workers.scriptName", operation: "eq", type: "string", value: "cb4-deployer" }, { kind: "group", filterCombination: "and", filters: [{ key: "at", operation: "starts_with", type: "string", value: "deploy." }] }] }
  });
  // 24 hours are sampled here and 6 are not: the span, two halves, four quarters.
  expect(rig.seen.map((b) => (b.timeframe.to - b.timeframe.from) / H)).toEqual([24, 12, 12, 6, 6, 6, 6]);
  expect(out.data.queries).toBe(7);
  expect([out.data.from, out.data.to]).toEqual([from, to]);
  // Each line once, oldest first, as the deployer wrote it with its time: nothing of the request Cloudflare keeps beside it.
  expect(out.data.lines.map((l) => [l.t - from, l.at, l.hash])).toEqual([[1 * H, "deploy.uploaded", "h1"], [1 * H + 5, "deploy.verify", "h1"], [12 * H, "deploy.failed", "h1"], [12 * H + 1, "deploy.put-back", "h1"], [23 * H, "deploy.verified", "h2"]]);
  expect(out.data.lines[2]).toEqual({ at: "deploy.failed", target: "brain-x-echo", hash: "h1", failed: ["test_a"], otherTestsRan: 132, ms: 8105, deployer: "f9007d2d", worker: "brain-deployer", ray: "a48bd6e14a6acc97", t: from + 12 * H });
  expect(JSON.stringify(out.data)).not.toMatch(/secret=1|\$workers|\$metadata|token\.minted|h9/);
  expect(out.data.workers).toEqual([{ worker: "brain-x-echo", runs: 2, verified: 1, failed: 1, none: 0, stale: 0, putBack: 1, hashes: [{ hash: "h1", verified: 0, failed: 1 }, { hash: "h2", verified: 1, failed: 0 }] }]);
  expect(out.data.tests).toEqual([{ worker: "brain-x-echo", test: "test_a", fails: 1, runs: 2, passedOnSameHash: 0, passedLater: 1, hashes: ["h1"], last: from + 12 * H }]);
  // With no span, the 24 hours before now.
  const now = await rig.get("");
  expect(now.data.to - now.data.from).toBe(24 * H);
  expect(Math.abs(now.data.to - Date.now())).toBeLessThan(600000);
  // A span that is not one, or longer than Cloudflare keeps, is refused before Cloudflare is asked.
  const asked = rig.seen.length;
  for (const bad of [`?from=${to}&to=${from}`, `?from=${from}&to=${from}`, "?from=yesterday", `?from=${from}&to=${from + 7 * 24 * H + 1}`, `?from=${from}&to=`]) expect([bad, (await rig.get(bad)).status]).toEqual([bad, 400]);
  expect(rig.seen.length).toBe(asked);
  // Cloudflare's refusal is an error of this method, with its status named.
  const down = await (await deploysRig(events, { status: 429 })).get(`?from=${from}&to=${to}`);
  expect([down.status, down.data.error]).toEqual([502, "UpstreamFailure"]);
  expect(down.data.message).toContain("429");
  return "5 lines in 7 queries";
}
// %%
test_deploy_lines_halves_a_window_until_it_is_whole = {
  const M = 60000, from = 1791676800000;
  const events = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7].map((i) => deployEvent(from + i * 10 * M, { at: "deploy.uploaded", target: "brain-x-w" + i, hash: "h" + i }));
  // A window that answers as many lines as the limit may hold more: it is halved, though nothing was sampled.
  const rig = await deploysRig(events, { whole: Infinity });
  const lines = await deployLines(async (body) => rig.answer(body), "cb4", from, from + 80 * M, { limit: 3 });
  expect(lines.map((l) => l.target)).toEqual(events.map((e) => e.source.target));
  expect(rig.seen.every((b) => b.limit === 3)).toBe(true);
  expect(rig.seen.length).toBeGreaterThan(3);
  // A minute that is still not whole is an error, not a short answer; so is a span that needs more queries than the bound.
  const crowded = await deploysRig([0, 1, 2].map((i) => deployEvent(from + i, { at: "deploy.uploaded", target: "brain-x-c" + i, hash: "h" })), { whole: Infinity });
  await expect(deployLines(async (body) => crowded.answer(body), "cb4", from, from + 80 * M, { limit: 3 })).rejects.toThrow(/not whole/);
  const sampled = await deploysRig(events, { whole: 0 });
  await expect(deployLines(async (body) => sampled.answer(body), "cb4", from, from + 7 * 24 * 60 * M, { queries: 20 })).rejects.toThrow(/more than 20 queries/);
  expect(sampled.seen.length).toBe(20);
  return rig.seen.length + " queries for 8 lines at 3 a window";
}
// %%
test_deploy_runs_counts_by_worker_hash_and_failed_test = {
  const line = (t, at, target, hash, more = {}) => ({ at, target, hash, t, ...more });
  const lines = [
    // The caret test of 2026-10-11: failed on one hash, passed on the next two. No pass on the hash it failed on.
    // Its put-back has no deploy.failed line before it, as on cb4 at 05:50:48 UTC: the put-back is the failed run.
    line(11, "deploy.put-back", "brain-x-issues", "i1", { previous: "i0", reason: "its tests failed: test_caret: expect(received).toEqual(expected)\n\nDifference; test_other: no" }),
    line(20, "deploy.verified", "brain-x-issues", "i2", { tests: 21 }),
    line(30, "deploy.verified", "brain-x-issues", "i3", { tests: 21 }),
    // One hash that failed and then passed with no change: the only evidence of a flake these lines hold.
    line(40, "deploy.failed", "brain-x-snapshot", "s1", { failed: ["test_days", "test_redraw"] }),
    line(41, "deploy.verified", "brain-x-snapshot", "s1", { tests: 9 }),
    // A failed run with its put-back, twice at one hash: two runs, not four.
    line(42, "deploy.failed", "brain-x-snapshot", "s2", { failed: ["test_redraw"] }),
    line(43, "deploy.put-back", "brain-x-snapshot", "s2", { previous: "s1", reason: "its tests failed: test_redraw: no" }),
    line(44, "deploy.failed", "brain-x-snapshot", "s2", { failed: ["test_redraw"] }),
    line(45, "deploy.put-back", "brain-x-snapshot", "s2", { previous: "s1", reason: "its tests failed: test_redraw: no" }),
    // No verdict, a verdict for another hash, a put-back with no test run, and a line with no Worker.
    line(50, "deploy.verify.none", "brain-x-git", "g1", { reason: "the page left no verdict" }),
    line(51, "deploy.verify.stale", "brain-x-git", "g0", { running: "g1" }),
    line(52, "deploy.put-back", "brain-x-git", "g1", { reason: "not confirmed in 10 minutes" }),
    line(60, "deploy.waiting", undefined, undefined),
    line(61, "deploy.uploaded", "brain-x-echo", "e1")
  ];
  // The order given does not matter: it is counted by time.
  const out = deployRuns([...lines].reverse());
  expect(out.workers).toEqual([
    { worker: "brain-x-git", runs: 0, verified: 0, failed: 0, none: 1, stale: 1, putBack: 1, hashes: [] },
    { worker: "brain-x-issues", runs: 3, verified: 2, failed: 1, none: 0, stale: 0, putBack: 1, hashes: [{ hash: "i1", verified: 0, failed: 1 }, { hash: "i2", verified: 1, failed: 0 }, { hash: "i3", verified: 1, failed: 0 }] },
    { worker: "brain-x-snapshot", runs: 4, verified: 1, failed: 3, none: 0, stale: 0, putBack: 2, hashes: [{ hash: "s1", verified: 1, failed: 1 }, { hash: "s2", verified: 0, failed: 2 }] }
  ]);
  expect(out.tests).toEqual([
    { worker: "brain-x-snapshot", test: "test_redraw", fails: 3, runs: 4, passedOnSameHash: 1, passedLater: 0, hashes: ["s1", "s2"], last: 44 },
    { worker: "brain-x-issues", test: "test_caret", fails: 1, runs: 3, passedOnSameHash: 0, passedLater: 1, hashes: ["i1"], last: 11 },
    { worker: "brain-x-snapshot", test: "test_days", fails: 1, runs: 4, passedOnSameHash: 1, passedLater: 0, hashes: ["s1"], last: 40 }
  ]);
  expect(deployRuns([])).toEqual({ workers: [], tests: [] });
  return "3 Workers, 3 tests";
}
// %%
import {cloudflare, cloudflareApi, config, simulate, decide, callerOf} from "@tomlarkworthy/cloudflare-iac"
// %%
import {plugins} from "@tomlarkworthy/plugin-registry"
// %%
import {expect} from "@tomlarkworthy/jest-expect-standalone"
